.TH MINIMAL-AIS-SERIAL 1
.SH NAME
minimal-ais-serial \- forward AIS serial messages to UDP destinations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B minimal-ais-serial
.B \-s
.I serial
[
.B \-b
.I baud
]
[
.B \-v
]
.B \-d
.I host:port[:Ns]
[
.B \-d
.I host:port[:Ns]
...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B minimal-ais-serial
reads NMEA AIS sentences from a serial port and forwards valid sentences to one
or more UDP destinations.
.PP
Validation is intentionally shallow. A sentence must start with
.B !
and have a valid NMEA checksum.
.PP
If a destination ends with
.I :Ns
the program rate limits that destination to one forwarded sentence per decoded
MMSI every
.I N
seconds. Sentences whose MMSI cannot be decoded are forwarded normally.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-s " serial"
Serial device path, for example
.IR /dev/ttyUSB0 .
.TP
.BI \-b " baud"
Serial baud rate. The default is
.BR 38400 .
.TP
.BI \-d " host:port[:Ns]"
UDP destination. Repeat this option for multiple destinations. IPv6 addresses
must be written as
.IR [address]:port .
.TP
.B \-v
Print each valid AIS sentence before forwarding it.
.TP
.B \-h
Show usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
minimal-ais-serial -s /dev/ttyUSB0 -b 38400 \e
    -d 127.0.0.1:10110 -d 10.10.10.255:10110:30s
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR minimal-ais-json (1)
